#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0333b70a-2ede-5c8d-b298-4d25ae4d4667" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication Rules"}] :question/id #uuid "b25256b2-09fe-5faa-b27a-bfea7654e8ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-3019" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^2} [3 \\mathbf f(t)+\\mathbf g(t)] \\,\\textrm{d}t$ given that $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^2} \\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t=\\left\\langle 16, \\: 2,\\: -2\\right\\rangle$ and $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^2}\\mathbf g(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t=\\left\\langle 2,-4,2\\right\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int_0^2} \\big[ \\, 3 \\mathbf f(t)+\\mathbf g(t) \\, \\big] \\, \\textrm d t = \\displaystyle {3\\int_0^2} \\mathbf f(t) \\, \\textrm d t + \\displaystyle {\\int_0^2} \\mathbf g(t) \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}[3f(t) + g(t)]dt &= 3∫_{0}^{2}f(t)dt + ∫_{0}^{2}g(t)dt \\\\\n&= 3⟨16, 2,-2⟩ + ⟨2,-4, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨48, 6,-6⟩ + ⟨2,-4, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨50, 2,-4⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5287f63-6d1a-5710-8a71-df5d1ba09a00" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Adjacent Intervals Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "5c4164d7-605a-597c-9bab-f6599becc527" :question/math-academy-id "e-3020" :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\ngiven that\n$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t= \\left\\langle \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\: 1, \\: 3 \\right\\rangle$\nand\n$\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^1\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t=\\left\\langle 0, \\: 2, \\: 6 \\right\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Using the adjacent intervals, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^1\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t=\\int_{-1}^0\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t+\\int_0^1\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t = \\int_{-1}^1\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t - \\int_0^1\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{0}f(t)dt &= ∫_{-1}^{1}f(t)dt - ∫_{0}^{1}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 2, 6⟩ - ⟨\\frac{3}{2}, 1, 3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\frac{3}{2}, 1, 3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119479 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c8ee97e-f2b0-587e-9171-8008f1e0c4aa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rules"}] :question/id #uuid "1d356bbb-b4ad-5730-9bd1-7f3cd151f96f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3021" :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle {\\int_0^1} \\mathbf c \\times \\mathbf f(t) \\, \\textrm d t$\ngiven that\n$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\mathbf f(t)\\,\\textrm d t = \\mathbf i-3\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k$\nand\n$\\mathbf c=\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k$." :question/worked-solution "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int_0^1} \\mathbf c \\times \\mathbf f(t) \\, \\textrm d t = \\mathbf c \\times {\\int_0^1} \\mathbf f(t) \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}c \\cdot f(t)dt &= c \\cdot ∫_{0}^{1}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= (j - k) \\cdot (i - 3j - 4k) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n&= (-4 - 3)i - (0 + 1)j + (0 - 1)k \\\\\n&=-7i - j - k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119622 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eb7fd4f-f4bc-535e-b6bf-0c7df387a375" :knowledge-point/title "Reversing the Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "40927582-03c4-5dcb-b2cc-7c68c73388b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8688" :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\mathbf f(t) + \\langle 1,\\:-1,\\:1\\rangle\\,\\textrm{d}t$\ngiven that\n$\\displaystyle\\int_2^0\\mathbf f(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t= \\left\\langle -3, \\: 2, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}f(t)dt &= - ∫_{2}^{0}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= - ⟨ - 3, 2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-2,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}f(t) + ⟨1,-1, 1⟩dt &= ∫_{0}^{2}f(t)dt + ∫_{0}^{2}⟨1,-1, 1⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-2,-1⟩ + ⟨t,-t, t⟩ \\mid _{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-2,-1⟩ + ⟨2,-2, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5,-4, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5287f63-6d1a-5710-8a71-df5d1ba09a00" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Adjacent Intervals Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a46d9991-cea8-530a-a916-54cff7bc8b6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 1,-3, 11⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119367} {:answer/id #uuid "a621b977-3ad4-5aec-9d97-262983cd5c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 10, 9,-11⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119368} {:answer/id #uuid "28be6686-1516-5dcd-a82a-05f02fac0ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨9, 1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119369} {:answer/id #uuid "484718db-f4b4-5553-9e94-b2aceefa69af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨7, 2, 11⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119370} {:answer/id #uuid "668c368d-fd3e-593e-948d-118a9d35e421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10, 6, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a621b977-3ad4-5aec-9d97-262983cd5c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 10, 9,-11⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119368} :answer-field/id #uuid "90abee0c-5f01-5329-8dee-f7e9a2b92b9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119366}] :question/id #uuid "29982efe-8588-56f9-8a2e-657a512dab79" :question/math-academy-id "q-96943" :question/problem "Calculate\n\n$∫_{1}^{2}f(t)dt$\ngiven that\n$∫_{1}^{3}f(t)dt = ⟨ - 2, 3, 4⟩$\nand\n$∫_{2}^{3}f(t)dt = ⟨8,-6, 15⟩$."}]}
